#{[17592186479797 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479797 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479797 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479797 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479710 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479797 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479797 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479797 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479797 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186479714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479797 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b34e94ad-ff95-5978-bc6c-c7f7116d7c1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479719 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479814 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479814 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479814 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186479698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479814 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e2e828d3-cf66-52fd-bbb8-8a49e2cdb0b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479824 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479824 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479824 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479824 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479824 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186479635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479824 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479824 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479824 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186479635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479824 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fe3e14eb-22b8-5147-9028-3893a72c3d27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479837 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479837 #edb/ref 1069 "q-14373" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479837 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479837 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{3+2x}{2x+3}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479837 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "155136a2-7453-5b52-882f-507c2e2bad0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479837 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we notice that $2x+3=3+2x.$ So we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3+2x}{2x+3}=\\frac{3+2x}{3+2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3+2x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3+2x}{3+2x} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{(3+2x)}}{\\cancel{(3+2x)}} & = \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479846 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479846 #edb/ref 1069 "q-100066" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479846 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479846 #edb/ref 1071 "What is $\\frac{yz}{3xz}$ reduced to lowest terms?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479846 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4044453a-5a6d-5fd7-b8c8-665b004cb36e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479846 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{yz}{3xz}=\\frac{y\\cdot z}{3\\cdot x\\cdot z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $z$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y\\cdot z}{3\\cdot x\\cdot z} & = \\\\ \\frac{y\\cdot \\cancel{z}}{3\\cdot x\\cdot \\cancel{z}} & = \\\\ \\frac{y}{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{y}{3x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479861 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479861 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67242" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479861 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479861 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{z(5+2z)}{3z}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479861 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "61eea428-2437-5847-999f-591f4a5a456c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479861 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{z(5+2z)}{3z}=\\frac{z\\cdot (5+2z)}{3\\cdot z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $z$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{z\\cdot (5+2z)}{3\\cdot z} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{z}\\cdot (5+2z)}{3\\cdot \\cancel{z}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5+2z}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{5+2z}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479868 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479868 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479868 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479868 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{12(x-1)}{15x}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479868 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "85ebb17c-ed44-5cf2-a5c7-233470157f33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479868 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $12(x-1)$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x-1),$ and the $15x$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot 5\\cdot x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12(x-1)}{15x}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x-1)}{3\\cdot 5\\cdot x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (x-1)}{3\\cdot 5\\cdot x} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\cdot (x-1)}{\\cancel{3}\\cdot 5\\cdot x} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot (x-1)}{5\\cdot x} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(x-1)}{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{4(x-1)}{5x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479873 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479873 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75370" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479873 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479873 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{18a}{9(a+2)}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479873 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8cbe3ef2-930d-574e-83d8-95fc23d584d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479873 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $18a$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 3\\cdot 3\\cdot a,$ and the $9(a+2)$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot 3\\cdot (a+2).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18a}{9(a+2)}=\\frac{2\\cdot 3\\cdot 3\\cdot a}{3\\cdot 3\\cdot (a+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there are two pairs of $3$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot 3\\cdot 3\\cdot a}{3\\cdot 3\\cdot (a+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\cdot a}{\\cancel{3}\\cdot \\cancel{3}\\cdot (a+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot a}{a+2} & = \\\\ \\frac{2a}{a+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{2a}{a+2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479878 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479878 #edb/ref 1069 "q-155" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479878 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479878 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{2(x-1)}{2(x+1)}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479878 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a06dc73c-225f-5c37-b7d5-563ea645e257" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479878 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $2(x-1)$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot (x-1),$ and the $2(x+1)$ in the denominator becomes $2\\cdot (x+1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(x-1)}{2(x+1)}=\\frac{2\\cdot (x-1)}{2\\cdot (x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is only one pair of $2$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot (x-1)}{2\\cdot (x+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot (x-1)}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot (x+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)}{(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{x-1}{x+1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479884 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479884 #edb/ref 1069 "q-41549" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479884 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479884 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{16}{20(y-3)}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479884 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b68074c4-93d7-57b0-8864-ec0076cbeea0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479884 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $16$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2,$ and the $20(y-3)$ in the denominator becomes $2\\cdot 2\\cdot 5\\cdot (y-3).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16}{20(y-3)}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 5\\cdot (y-3)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there are two pairs of $2$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 5\\cdot (y-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot \\cancel{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot \\cancel{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot (y-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2}{5\\cdot (y-3)} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{5(y-3)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{4}{5(y-3)}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479885 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479885 #edb/ref 1069 "q-75375" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479885 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479885 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{3a(a-b)}{9(a-b)}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479885 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b735757b-2dac-511b-87bc-3911444d7136" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479885 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $3a(a-b)$ in the numerator becomes $3\\cdot a\\cdot (a-b),$ and the $9(a-b)$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot 3\\cdot (a-b).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3a(a-b)}{9(a-b)}=\\frac{3\\cdot a\\cdot (a-b)}{3\\cdot 3\\cdot (a-b)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $3$'s and one pair of $(a-b)$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3a(a-b)}{9(a-b)} & =\\frac{3\\cdot a\\cdot (a-b)}{3\\cdot 3\\cdot (a-b)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{3}\\cdot a\\cdot \\cancel{(a-b)}}{\\cancel{3}\\cdot 3\\cdot \\cancel{(a-b)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{a}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{a}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479894 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479894 #edb/ref 1069 "q-41557" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479894 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479894 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{32x(5z+1)}{12xz}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479894 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d8919a5c-5e3b-5274-9d2f-901afddd5e90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479894 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $32x(5z+1)$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot (5z+1),$ and the $12xz$ in the denominator becomes $3\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot z.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{32x(5z+1)}{12xz}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot (5z+1)}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot z}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there are two pairs of $2\\text{'s}$ and one pair of $x$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot (5z+1)}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot x\\cdot z} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot \\cancel{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\cancel{x}\\cdot (5z+1)}{3\\cdot \\cancel{2}\\cdot \\cancel{2}\\cdot \\cancel{x}\\cdot z} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2\\cdot (5z+1)}{3\\cdot z} & = \\\\ \\frac{8(5z+1)}{3z}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{8(5z+1)}{3z}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479901 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479901 #edb/ref 1069 "q-41552" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479901 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479901 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{(3x)(7y)}{28y}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479901 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fad200a4-e2f6-57c9-aa77-2766fc482dc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479901 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $(3x)(7y)$ in the numerator becomes $3\\cdot 7\\cdot x\\cdot y,$ and the $28y$ in the denominator becomes $2\\cdot 2\\cdot 7\\cdot y.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(3x)(7y)}{28y}=\\frac{3\\cdot 7\\cdot x\\cdot y}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 7\\cdot y}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is one pair of $7\\text{'s}$ and one pair of $y$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\cdot 7\\cdot x\\cdot y}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 7\\cdot y} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{7}\\cdot x\\cdot \\cancel{y}}{2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\cancel{7}\\cdot \\cancel{y}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot x}{2\\cdot 2} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{3}{4}x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
